The hook is widely recognized as a key element for video ads, but much of the existing advice revolved around the same handful of hook styles, determined by experts who aren't explaining how important it is to tailor those broader hook categories into specific formats that work for your brand. That is the purpose of this resource.

Learn how to develop your own hooks and cultivate styles that drive results around relevant audiences.

One of the first ideas I'd like to broach, is the difference between visual and verbal/audio hooks. Is there one, and how does it impact performance? The answer is (as it often is) that it varies. 

Visual hooks and verbal hooks can both help stop the scroll. Depending on your core demo and the ad platform, impacts may vary considering factors such as attention, whether sound is on, personal relevance, and entertainment value. At the end of the day, the viewer needs to feel that they'll get something from the 30s-1m you're asking them to commit to watch your ad. Hooks tend to be 3-5s and most times that's all you have to convince someone to watch further. 

Some of the most common "hooks" I have seen include:
* **Curiousity:** Starts with a question or information "gap" that invites users to keep watching for the answer
* **Negative/Contrarian:** Uses negative language to challenge the user to keep watching, or opposes a commmon thought or process to suggest a better way
* **Problem:** Either phrases a problem (with the aim to provide a solution) or invites the user to learn more about a problem they may have
* **Social Proof:** Often grouped under UGC, demonstrates that other people enjoy the brand (suggesting you will too)
* **Authority:** Sets the avatar or speaker as an expert in their field, and leads into what they found or recommend.
* **Context:** Requires both the visual and the on-screen text to communicate the hook
* **Shocking/Surprise:** Relies on lesser known information, or even startling content to capture attention

These can all be shaped into visual or verbal hooks. But there are so many nuances, for instance a visual hook can rely on any of the following: image/set of immages, a video, static & dynamic collage, on-screen captions. A verbal hook can be a trending or custom sound, or V.O. Then, there is the possibility of using music as a hook, which I'd argue brings us from verbal to a wider world of audio hooks. Many times visual/verbal/audio won't be as effective alone, even when you're not relying on context-based hooks.

Then as we go deeper, we arrive at the realization that all of the above categorizations are incredibly generic. They could all be taken a number of ways and beyond the visual/verbal combo, multiple hook categories could be combined as well.

The approach you might use to hook a viewer's attention for organic, may not work for ads or may attract the wrong set of users (ones who are unlikely to take your desired action, regardless of how strong the later two compnents of your video are)

That's the joy of testing and learning. The possibilities are endless, but intentional testing can help steer you towards the concepts that work best, and can hopefully inspire new iterations of failed hook types, or net new approaches to getting a user to stop scrolling for your video.

Below, I am going to outline some more niche hook examples, which broader categories they may bubble up to, and how they might inspire future tests. All of this context can help you start to consider the ways you might breakdown your existing content and/or build anew.
* **Organic Intercept:** Mirror the visual or verbal styles of organic content your audience is already watching naatively. This opens a whole world of persona-based testing.
* **Product Reaction:** Could be a niche of social proof, or not. The goal is to showcase an emotional reaction (i.e., excitement, disappoinment) which can, but doesn't need to be, paired with a verbal or audio accompaniment. This could also shape the video body (i.e., a taste test, a shopping haul, a collection overview)
* **Partner Claims:** Leaning on a partner or product to capture interest, rather than your brand or "customers" Typically you are framing the product of the partnership as unique, or setting it apart from something else. What differentiates this hook, is the brand POV. Rather than boosting an ad directly patting your own brand on the pack, you are supporting partners, who may or may not be well known. This could influence viewer response/sentiment, and depending on popularity or common knowledge, it could pair well with an educational body.
* **Comment Response:** A popular visual hook that infuses authority, question, problem, and social proof. You directly address a customer question or ad comment (good/bad/random) and the resulting body should provide the viewer with a reason to consider your product/brand.
* **Rapid Montage:** This is a visual hook that would likely perform better when paired with a strong V.O. or a sound that builds context. It is a way to get views, but is typically not strong enough on its own to pull a high intent viewer
* **Unboxing:** This is a style of content, and most would categorize it as Social Proof, but I would argue it also functions well as a standalone hook. UGC is incredibly strong and if you deliver to customers, then you can capitalize on the average person's love of watching hauls. I've seen this done in so many ways, from a plain introduction (Today I am going to unbox...) to a product reaction, to a birds eye view or vlog-style delivery day opener. There are so many ways to play with this format, and it truly is a disservice to bulk multiple hook styles under Social Proof. 

